WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.807
File security does not end when the upload
succeeds.

2
00:00:02.887 --> 00:00:06.243
Upload authorization and download
authorization are separate decisions.

3
00:00:06.323 --> 00:00:08.630
A user who can upload a document for one
case, tenant,

4
00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:12.171
or account should not automatically be
able to download every file in storage.

5
00:00:12.251 --> 00:00:15.240
Before serving a file, generating a signed
URL, creating a preview,

6
00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:19.042
or exposing metadata, the application
should enforce object-level authorization

7
00:00:19.122 --> 00:00:20.381
against the business model.

8
00:00:20.461 --> 00:00:22.663
Avoid predictable public URLs for private
files.

9
00:00:22.743 --> 00:00:24.316
Storage keys, object identifiers,

10
00:00:24.396 --> 00:00:27.700
and signed URLs need careful control
because they can become access paths.

11
00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:30.768
A signed URL may be appropriate for a
short-lived private download,

12
00:00:30.848 --> 00:00:34.414
but it should be scoped, time-limited, and
generated only after authorization.

13
00:00:34.494 --> 00:00:37.588
A public bucket or web root may be
appropriate for truly public assets,

14
00:00:37.668 --> 00:00:41.128
but it is usually a poor default for
customer documents, support attachments,

15
00:00:41.208 --> 00:00:43.044
tax forms, resumes, or internal imports.

16
00:00:43.124 --> 00:00:44.487
Metadata can be sensitive too.

17
00:00:44.567 --> 00:00:48.028
Original filename, uploader identity,
upload time, file size, content type,

18
00:00:48.108 --> 00:00:50.624
scan status, tenant ID, storage key,
processing result,

19
00:00:50.704 --> 00:00:53.116
and retention state can reveal business
information.

20
00:00:53.196 --> 00:00:56.710
Metadata endpoints and previews should
follow the same authorization model as

21
00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:57.314
downloads.

22
00:00:57.394 --> 00:01:00.645
Do not reflect untrusted filenames into
headers, HTML, templates, logs,

23
00:01:00.725 --> 00:01:02.665
or JSON responses without proper handling.

24
00:01:02.745 --> 00:01:05.786
Download responses should use appropriate
headers for the use case,

25
00:01:05.866 --> 00:01:09.379
including safe content disposition and
content type behavior where relevant.

26
00:01:09.459 --> 00:01:13.235
The application should avoid surprising
inline rendering of untrusted content when

27
00:01:13.315 --> 00:01:14.154
download is safer.

28
00:01:14.234 --> 00:01:17.589
File deletion, ownership transfer,
sharing, export, preview generation,

29
00:01:17.669 --> 00:01:20.344
and retention should follow explicit
authorization rules.

30
00:01:20.424 --> 00:01:22.993
Retrieval is often where sensitive data
exposure occurs,

31
00:01:23.073 --> 00:01:25.380
so treat it as a first-class part of
upload security.
